; pi4SdioProbe.pi4 - the second SD controller, the SDIO bus on it, and
; the CYW43455 radio at the end of it. Everything that can be learned
; about the Pi 4's WiFi part WITHOUT firmware, printed in one pass.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
; This is the artefact tools/a64/a64_sdio_check.py runs. Its output
; format is therefore load-bearing: the gate reads values back out of
; the serial log by label, so a label changed here changes the gate.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT IT DOES, AND THE ORDER MATTERS
; ======================================================================
;   1. THE FIRMWARE HALF. The power-device table, the EMMC clock's
;      state, and WL_ON. All three go through the property mailbox and
;      all three are now sdio.pi4's own code rather than this file's -
;      this file calls SdioAssertWlOn() and prints the ladder it left
;      behind. See THE POWER RAIL in RaspberryPi4/Lib/sdio.pi4 for why
;      that moved and what it cost to leave it here.
;
;   2. THE BASE CLOCK, from the mailbox, for clock id 1 (EMMC). NOT 12
;      (EMMC2) - those are different clocks on this chip and only one of
;      them feeds the controller at $FE300000. sdio.pi4 refuses rather
;      than guessing, which is why this step exists.
;
;   3. THE RAW REGISTERS, PRINTED BEFORE ANY OPINION IS FORMED. Host
;      Controller Version and both Capabilities words for this
;      controller, and the same three for EMMC2 next door as a control.
;      This is new, and it is the whole lesson of the first bench run:
;      the diagnostic printed a confident wrong conclusion and none of
;      the evidence, so the evidence had to be read out of a monitor
;      prompt by hand before the conclusion could be seen to be
;      wrong. Three extra lines would have saved that.
;
;   4. SdioInit() - reset, power, 400 kHz, CMD0, CMD5, CMD3, CMD7, 25
;      MHz, four-bit bus.
;
;   5. Enable SDIO functions 1 and 2.
;
;   6. Cyw43Attach() - block sizes, the ALP clock sequence, the chip ID,
;      the core walk.
;
;   7. BLOCK-MODE CMD53, COMPARED AGAINST BYTE MODE. Added 2026-08-26,
;      and it is the only section here that is an EXPERIMENT rather than
;      a report. One region of the chip is read twice in byte mode - the
;      control, which proves the region reads the same twice - and once
;      in block mode, and the three results are diffed. Section 5c says
;      what each outcome means.
;
;   8. THE CORES GO DOWN AND THE CHIP IS ASKED HOW MUCH RAM IT HAS.
;      The first WRITES this program has ever made to the radio. The ARM
;      CR4 is brought out of reset but HALTED, the 802.11 core is
;      disabled, and the CR4's memory banks are walked for a RAM size.
;      Then a 256-byte pattern is written into that RAM and read back.
;
;   9. Ask for the things that STILL cannot work, and print the
;      refusals. That is not padding: it is the honest state of the
;      work. The list is shorter than it was - firmware upload and start
;      are implemented as of 2026-08-26 - and what remains is refused
;      because the SDPCM transport is not written, NOT because of a
;      licence. See THE FIRMWARE in RaspberryPi4/Lib/cyw43.pi4.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT THIS PROGRAM STILL CANNOT DO, AND IT IS ARITHMETIC, NOT PERMISSION
; ======================================================================
; It does not upload firmware, because it has nowhere to upload FROM.
; The image is 609,309 bytes and this diagnostic is a few tens of
; kilobytes; the blob cannot be compiled into it. The image and the CLM
; blob are in the repository at Firmware/CYW43455/ (the NVRAM is board
; data and is not) and the library's
; Cyw43UploadFirmware takes a pointer and a length, so what is missing
; is a way to get 609 KB into the Pi's own RAM at run time. There are
; two, and choosing between them is a separate job with its own risks:
;
;   * fat.pi4 reads real FAT32 off the boot medium and is proven on
;     silicon. The host copies the three files onto the stick once.
;     WRITING to that stick can damage the boot image; READING cannot,
;     and reading is all this needs.
;
;   * Anvil's serial `receive` puts them in RAM over the wire. Slower,
;     needs no change to the stick, and needs no new code in the radio
;     path at all.
;
; Either way cyw43.pi4 does not care which, and this program is
; deliberately not the one that chooses.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHERE IT STOPPED LAST TIME, AND WHAT EACH STOPPING POINT MEANS
; ======================================================================
; THE FIRST RUN ON SILICON WAS 2026-08-26 AND IT STOPPED AT THE BUS:
;
;     mailbox init (want 1)    1
;     WL_ON asserted (want 1)  0
;     EMMC clock Hz (id 1)     250000000
;     SdioInit (want 1)        0
;     sdio: Capabilities register all-zero or all-ones - wrong device?
;     last cmd $FF  last intr $00000000  last state $000F0001
;
; BOTH DIAGNOSES IN THAT LOG WERE WRONG. Both are fixed.
;
;   THE CAPABILITIES MESSAGE sent the bench looking at the base address,
;   and the base address was right. Read by hand afterwards: $FE3000FC
;   returned $99020000, which is Host Controller Version $9902 - spec
;   version 2, SDHCI 3.00, vendor $99 - from a block that is plainly
;   decoding, while EMMC2 at $FE3400FC returned $10020000 as a control.
;   An all-zero Capabilities register on THIS controller is normal:
;   Raspberry Pi Linux rpi-6.12.y drivers/mmc/host/bcm2835-mmc.c, the driver
;   Raspberry Pi binds to this node, reads SDHCI_CAPABILITIES exactly
;   once (lines 255-257) and only to print it in a debug dump. sdio.pi4
;   no longer refuses over it.
;
;   THE 250000000 IS NOT EVIDENCE THAT THE CLOCK IS RUNNING either, and
;   this file used to imply that it was. GET_CLOCK_RATE returns the rate
;   a clock WOULD run at - "Next enable rate should be returned even if
;   the clock is not running",
;   the Raspberry Pi firmware's Mailbox property interface document,
;   line 310 of the copy read.
;   The on/off fact is GET_CLOCK_STATE bit 0, and it is printed now.
;
;   THE WL_ON FAILURE had two candidate causes in this file's own nine
;   lines and both were real bugs. GET_GPIO_CONFIG was asked for with a
;   twenty-four byte payload, but struct gpio_get_config is five words -
;   twenty - and only struct gpio_set_config is six (expander driver
;   lines 32-47). And the direction word was written as 0, which is
;   RPI_EXP_GPIO_DIR_IN (line 22), so the pin was being configured as an
;   INPUT while a state field it would then ignore said 1. Both are
;   fixed, in sdio.pi4, which now owns the whole sequence and power-
;   cycles the pin the way the bench-proven Pico driver does.
;
; THE REMAINING STOPPING POINTS, in the order they would be met:
;
;   "the word at $FE3000FC is all-zero or all-ones"
;       NOW this really is the base address or the low-peripheral
;       translation, because that word answered on 2026-08-26 and this
;       message no longer fires for anything else. Compare against
;       uart.pi4 reaching the PL011 at $FE201000, which has printed on
;       this bench, and against the EMMC2 control rows this file prints
;       directly above it.
;
;   "nothing answered CMD5"
;       Read the WL_ON ladder at the top. Step 9 with level 1 means the
;       firmware says the radio is powered and the fault is further
;       along - the pin mux, or the bus. Anything less than 9 names the
;       transaction that failed; SdioWlOnStep in sdio.pi4 lists what
;       each rung means.
;
;   "CHIPCLKCSR did not read back what was written"
;       The SDIO layer works and function 1 does not. Almost always the
;       function enable, occasionally the block size.
;
;   "chip ID reads all-zero"
;       ALP never really came up, or the backplane window registers went
;       somewhere else. The window is the likeliest: check that the
;       address written to SBADDRLOW is the address SHIFTED RIGHT EIGHT.
;
;   pmfc pi4SdioProbe.pi4 -t pi4 --load-addr 0x400000 \
;        --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns -o sdio.img

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/sdio.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/cyw43_rx_glom.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/cyw43.pi4"

; ======================================================================
;  WHAT THIS FILE NO LONGER OWNS
;
;  It used to carry its own nine-line probe_WlOn() and its own copies of
;  the expander constants. Both are gone and sdio.pi4 has them, because
;  a bus driver whose device is unpowered is not a driver and because
;  the diagnostic and the library had two chances to get the payload
;  sizes right and got the same one wrong twice.
;
;  The constants that MOVED, with their citations, are in
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/sdio.pi4 near #SDIO_TAG_GET_POWER_STATE. The
;  procedure that replaced probe_WlOn() is SdioAssertWlOn().
;
;  WHAT STAYS HERE is the sweep - see probe_PowerTable below - because
;  it is a question a diagnostic asks once and a library should never
;  ask at all.
; ======================================================================

; Mailbox clock id 1 = EMMC.
; U-Boot v2025.01 arch/arm/mach-bcm283x/include/mach/mbox.h line 229.
#PROBE_CLOCK_ID_EMMC = 1

; Mailbox clock id 12 = EMMC2, the micro-SD controller's clock. Printed
; alongside id 1 ONLY as a control, exactly as the EMMC2 registers are:
; two clocks, one of which is known to be feeding a working controller.
; U-Boot v2025.01 arch/arm/mach-bcm283x/include/mach/mbox.h line 239.
#PROBE_CLOCK_ID_EMMC2 = 12

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  AND EMMC2's THREE REGISTERS ARE **NOT** DEFINED HERE, DELIBERATELY.
;
;  They would be the perfect control - $FE3400FC read $10020000 and
;  $FE340040 read $45EE6432 on 2026-08-26, which is what proved our MMIO
;  reads were fine and turned the Capabilities verdict over - and this
;  program still does not read them. Two reasons, and the second is the
;  real one:
;
;    * sdio.pi4's header states the invariant plainly: "Nothing in this
;      file touches $FE340000 and nothing in emmc.pi4 touches
;      $FE300000." A diagnostic that quietly reaches into the other
;      controller's window is the first crack in that, and the window it
;      would reach into belongs to the boot medium.
;
;    * tools/a64/a64_sdio_check.py enforces it. Any access outside the
;      mmcnr node's declared window aborts the gate with "if that is
;      inside another SD controller's window, the library has the wrong
;      base address". That check is worth more than three log lines.
;
;  So the control rows are read from the monitor prompt instead, by
;  hand, and the addresses are printed by this program so that they
;  never have to be looked up again. See probe_RawRegisters.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; The three registers this program IS entitled to read. ARM
; $FE300000, from mmcnr@7e300000 - Raspberry Pi Linux rpi-6.12.y
; arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm270x.dtsi lines 50-60. The offsets are
; SDHCI's:
; SDHCI_CAPABILITIES $40 and SDHCI_CAPABILITIES_1 $44
; (rpi-6.12.y drivers/mmc/host/sdhci.h lines 210 and 231), and
; SDHCI_HOST_VERSION is the
; 16-bit register at $FE, so the whole word at $FC carries it in its top
; half. READ ONLY, here. Everything that writes to this window is
; sdio.pi4's.
#PROBE_SDIO_VER  = $FE3000FC
#PROBE_SDIO_CAP0 = $FE300040
#PROBE_SDIO_CAP1 = $FE300044


; ======================================================================
;  THE TWO COMPARISON BUFFERS, and why there are two of them.
;
;  Every experiment below reads the same region of the chip twice and
;  diffs the results, so it needs two buffers that exist at the same
;  time. They are Dim arrays of .l because a Dim array lands 8-byte
;  aligned on this backend - more than the 4 that CMD53's contract asks
;  for - which is the same note fat.pi4 makes about its sector buffers.
;
;  256 BYTES IS FOUR OF FUNCTION 1'S 64-BYTE BLOCKS, which is the
;  smallest size that exercises what actually had to be written today:
;  one block proves nothing about the per-block re-arm, two proves the
;  loop runs twice, and four is enough that an off-by-one in the block
;  arithmetic lands somewhere visible. It is also small enough to sit
;  comfortably inside the EROM table, which the read comparison depends
;  on - see section 5c.
; ======================================================================
#PROBE_XFER_BYTES = 256
Global Dim probe_bufA.l[#PROBE_XFER_BYTES / 4]
Global Dim probe_bufB.l[#PROBE_XFER_BYTES / 4]


Procedure PutHexN(v.i, digits.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = digits - 1 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(48 + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure Line(*name, v.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  PrintDec(v)
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure HexLine(*name, v.i, digits.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  $")
  PutHexN(v, digits)
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  probe_Count - print a comparison result that is allowed to say it
;  never happened.
;
;  A NEGATIVE COUNT MEANS NOT COMPARED, and it prints as those words
;  rather than as a number. This exists because of one line in the
;  2026-08-26 bench log:
;
;      control mismatches (want 0)  0
;
;  printed while both of the reads it was comparing had failed and
;  nothing at all had been walked. Zero mismatches out of zero bytes is
;  arithmetically true, reads as a pass, and was the single most
;  misleading line in the run. Every count in this program that can be
;  skipped now goes through here.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure probe_Count(*name, v.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  If v < 0
    UartWriteStr("NOT COMPARED - the reads it needed did not both succeed")
  Else
    PrintDec(v)
  EndIf
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure TextLine(*name, *value)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*name)
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*value)
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  probe_PowerTable - GET_POWER_STATE for every device id any source on
;  this disk names, printed as a table.
;
;  THIS IS HERE BECAUSE NO FILE ON THIS DISK ANSWERS THE QUESTION, and a
;  single bench run does. The two sources disagree about what device id
;  0 even is:
;
;      U-Boot v2025.01 arch/arm/mach-bcm283x/include/mach/mbox.h line 177
;          #define BCM2835_MBOX_POWER_DEVID_SDHCI   0
;      the firmware's Mailbox property interface document, line 168
;          * 0x00000000: SD Card
;
;  and the same document lists ids 9 and 10 as "Unknown (RPi4)" at lines
;  177-178, which is a written admission that the table was never
;  updated for this part. The reply's bit 1 is the firmware saying "no
;  such device" (lines 190-193), so a sweep separates the ids that exist
;  on a BCM2711 from the ids that were only ever BCM2835's.
;
;  Nothing is set. This reads.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure probe_PowerTable()
  Define id.i
  Define s.i

  For id = 0 To 10
    s = SdioQueryPowerState(id)
    UartWriteStr("  power devid ")
    PrintDec(id)
    UartWriteStr("  state ")
    PrintDec(s)
    If s < 0
      UartWriteStr("  (not asked - mailbox refused)")
    Else
      If (s & #SDIO_MBX_STATE_NODEV) <> 0
        ; Bit 1 of a RESPONSE means the device does not exist. In a
        ; SET_POWER_STATE REQUEST the same bit means "wait for the
        ; rail"; it is only a no-such-device flag on the way back. Both
        ; constants are sdio.pi4's, with the citations attached there.
        UartWriteStr("  no such device")
      Else
        If (s & #SDIO_MBX_STATE_ON) <> 0
          UartWriteStr("  exists, ON")
        Else
          UartWriteStr("  exists, off")
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf
    PrintNl()
  Next
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  probe_RawRegisters - the three words that decide whether the block is
;  there, for BOTH SDHCI-class controllers, before anything judges them.
;
;  THIS IS THE FIX FOR THE FIRST BENCH RUN. The log said "wrong device?"
;  and printed four status words, none of which was the register the
;  message was about. The two numbers that settled it - $FE3000FC and
;  $FE300040 - had to be read out of a monitor prompt afterwards. A
;  diagnostic that names a register in its verdict must print that
;  register.
;
;  IT READS THE ADDRESSES ITSELF rather than calling sdio.pi4's
;  accessors, and that is deliberate: those accessors hold nothing until
;  SdioInit() has run, and the whole point of these three rows is to
;  exist BEFORE the library has formed an opinion. It is also a second,
;  independent reading of the same window by a second file, which is
;  worth something on a bring-up where the address itself was the
;  suspect for a day.
;
;  THE CONTROL IS NAMED, NOT READ. See the comment beside
;  #PROBE_SDIO_VER for why this program will not touch $FE34xxxx. The
;  three addresses are printed so the next person can paste them into
;  the monitor instead of deriving them, and the values recorded on
;  2026-08-26 are printed beside them so a disagreement is visible
;  without going back through the handoffs.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  probe_FirmwareHalfReport - everything SdioInit's firmware half did,
;  printed on the success path and the failure path alike.
;
;  THE ATTEMPT, NOT JUST THE AFTERMATH. The first bench run reported
;  "clock state before 0 / clock state after 0" and that single pair is
;  compatible with three completely different stories: the set was never
;  sent, the set was sent and the firmware refused it, or the set was
;  accepted and the clock did not move. Each needs a different next
;  step, and the log could not choose between them. So the reply word
;  and the mailbox error from the set itself are printed here.
;
;  A REPLY OF -1 MEANS THE TRANSACTION NEVER COMPLETED. It is not a
;  state word. The mailbox error on the next line is then the reason,
;  and #MBX_ERR_TAG (8) in particular means the firmware came back and
;  declined to service the tag at all.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure probe_FirmwareHalfReport()
  HexLine("host version raw ", SdioHostVersionRaw(), 8)
  HexLine("capabilities 0   ", SdioCaps0(), 8)
  HexLine("capabilities 1   ", SdioCaps1(), 8)
  Line("caps read as zero", SdioCapsAreZero())
  Line("mailbox was used ", SdioMailboxUsed())
  Line("clock state before", SdioClockStateBefore())
  Line("clock set reply  ", SdioClockSetReply())
  Line("clock set mbx err", SdioClockSetError())
  TextLine("clock set meaning", SdioMailboxErrorText(SdioClockSetError()))
  ; The control for the same question: a SET-type tag that has been seen
  ; to PASS the per-tag check on this firmware. If this reads 8 bytes
  ; with MBX_OK while the two GPIO sets read 0 bytes with MBX_ERR_TAG,
  ; the two cases are genuinely different and mailbox.pi4's strictness
  ; is not on its own the explanation.
  Line("clock set resp bytes", SdioClockSetRespBytes())
  Line("clock state after", SdioClockStateAfter())
  Line("clock Hz measured", SdioClockMeasured())
  ; Read-only as of the second bench run. See sdio_EnableClockAndPower:
  ; the firmware reported UART0 powered off while UART0 was printing,
  ; so this file no longer acts on the power table. Before and after are
  ; the same query and will agree; both are printed because a future
  ; firmware that starts maintaining the table would show up here as a
  ; disagreement, and that would be worth noticing.
  Line("power state before", SdioPowerStateBefore())
  Line("power state after", SdioPowerStateAfter())
  Line("WL_ON step       ", SdioWlOnStep())
  Line("WL_ON level      ", SdioWlOnLevel())
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  probe_DecodeIntr - the Interrupt Status word, one named bit per line.
;
;  THE BENCH SHOULD NEVER HAVE TO DECODE $00048000 BY HAND AGAIN. It did,
;  on 2026-08-26, and the decode was correct but it needed the header
;  open beside the log. The names come from sdio.pi4's SdioIntBitName,
;  which carries a line number from rpi-6.12.y_sdhci.h against every one
;  of them.
;
;  Only the bits that are SET and that the header actually names are
;  printed. A run with a clean status word prints one line saying so,
;  which is more useful than thirty-two rows of nothing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure probe_DecodeIntr(v.i)
  Define i.i
  Define shown.i

  shown = 0
  For i = 0 To 31
    If ((v >> i) & 1) = 1
      If SdioIntBitIsNamed(i) = 1
        UartWriteStr("    bit ")
        PrintDec(i)
        UartWriteStr("  ")
        UartWriteStr(SdioIntBitName(i))
        PrintNl()
        shown = shown + 1
      Else
        UartWriteStr("    bit ")
        PrintDec(i)
        UartWriteStr("  set, and the header names no such bit - suspect a bad read")
        PrintNl()
        shown = shown + 1
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Next
  If shown = 0
    UartWriteStr("    no interrupt status bits are set")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  probe_DecodeR5 - the CMD52/CMD53 response, one named bit per line,
;  and the reason it exists is the same as probe_DecodeIntr's.
;
;  ON 2026-08-26 THIS PROGRAM PRINTED "the card REFUSED the command -
;  see SdioLastR5" AND STOPPED THERE. That sentence tells the reader that
;  something is wrong and then asks for a search to find out what, from a
;  variable, with the header open. The five R5 status bits are five
;  unrelated faults - a length the function will not take, a function
;  that is not enabled, an illegal command, a CRC failure - and knowing
;  which one it was is the whole diagnosis. Printing the number and not
;  the meaning is the instrument failing, not the bus.
;
;  Always prints the raw word too. A decoder can only name the bits its
;  table knows; the raw value is what survives a table that is wrong.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure probe_DecodeR5(v.i)
  Define i.i
  Define shown.i

  UartWriteStr("    R5 raw $")
  PutHexN(v, 4)
  UartWriteStr("   data byte $")
  PutHexN(v & $FF, 2)
  PrintNl()

  shown = 0
  For i = 8 To 15
    If ((v >> i) & 1) = 1
      If SdioR5BitIsNamed(i) = 1
        UartWriteStr("    bit ")
        PrintDec(i)
        UartWriteStr("  ")
        UartWriteStr(SdioR5BitName(i))
        PrintNl()
        shown = shown + 1
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Next
  If shown = 0
    UartWriteStr("    no R5 status bit is set - the card did NOT refuse, so")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("    whatever failed, it was not the card objecting")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  probe_XferFailed - everything worth knowing about a failed bulk
;  transfer, in one call, from both layers.
;
;  THE TWO LAYERS DISAGREE ABOUT WHAT WENT WRONG AND BOTH ARE RIGHT.
;  cyw43.pi4 says "a bulk transfer failed" because that is all it knows;
;  sdio.pi4 says which command, which block and which status bits. A log
;  that carries only the first sends the reader to the wrong file.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure probe_XferFailed()
  UartWriteStr("    ")
  UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("    ")
  UartWriteStr(SdioErrorText())
  PrintNl()
  Line("    failed on block ", SdioLastXferBlock())
  probe_DecodeR5(SdioLastR5())
  HexLine("    last intr       ", SdioLastIntr(), 8)
  probe_DecodeIntr(SdioLastIntr())
  HexLine("    present state   ", SdioLastStatus(), 8)
EndProcedure



; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  probe_BusRegisters - what the controller's own registers hold, read
;  back rather than assumed.
;
;  The bench asked for the divider that was ACTUALLY PROGRAMMED, which
;  is a different claim from the divider this code computed. Both are
;  printed, on the same screen, so a disagreement between them is
;  visible instead of inferred.
;
;  THE PULL REGISTER IS HERE FOR A REASON. It held b10 - PULL DOWN - on
;  CMD and DAT0..3 for the whole of this bring-up, because the device
;  tree numbers pull-up as 2 and the BCM2711 register numbers 2 as
;  pull-down. Bits 15:4 of the value below should now read $555 in
;  their upper five fields: b01 five times for GPIO35..39, and b00 for
;  GPIO34. See THE PULL ENCODING IS INVERTED in sdio.pi4.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure probe_BusRegisters()
  HexLine("control0 ($28)   ", SdioControl0Raw(), 8)
  HexLine("control1 ($2C)   ", SdioControl1Raw(), 8)
  Line("divisor computed ", SdioDivisor())
  Line("divisor in reg   ", SdioDivisorProgrammed())
  Line("base clock kHz   ", SdioBaseClockKhz())
  Line("bus clock kHz    ", SdioClockKhz())
  HexLine("gpfsel3          ", SdioGpfsel3(), 8)
  HexLine("gppup2 (want $550)", SdioGppup2() & $0000FFF0, 8)
EndProcedure


Procedure probe_RawRegisters()
  HexLine("sdio  $FE3000FC ver ", PeekN(#PROBE_SDIO_VER), 8)
  HexLine("sdio  $FE300040 cap0", PeekN(#PROBE_SDIO_CAP0), 8)
  HexLine("sdio  $FE300044 cap1", PeekN(#PROBE_SDIO_CAP1), 8)
  UartWriteStr("  control, read these from the monitor, not from here:")
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("    $FE3400FC was $10020000 and $FE340040 was $45EE6432")
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("    on 2026-08-26 - EMMC2, the micro-SD controller")
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure


Procedure.i Main()
  Define ok.i
  Define hz.i
  Define khz.i
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  Define bad.i
  Define ctl.i
  Define okA.i
  Define okB.i
  Define okC.i

  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("=== pi4 SDIO / CYW43455 probe ===")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 1 - the mailbox, then WL_ON
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- power and clock --")
  PrintNl()

  ok = MailboxInit()
  Line("mailbox init (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  mailbox refused - nothing below can be trusted")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; --- the power-device table ------------------------------------------
  ; Before anything is switched on, ask the firmware what it thinks
  ; exists. See probe_PowerTable: this is the question the documents on
  ; this disk contradict each other about.
  probe_PowerTable()
  PrintNl()

  ; --- WL_ON, now sdio.pi4's job ---------------------------------------
  ; SdioInit() calls this too and calling it twice is harmless. It is
  ; called here as well so that the ladder is printed BEFORE the bus
  ; work, which is where a reader will look first, and so that the
  ; 170 ms this spends power-cycling the radio is spent before the
  ; controller is touched rather than in the middle of it.
  ok = SdioAssertWlOn()
  Line("WL_ON asserted (want 1)", ok)
  Line("WL_ON step (want 13)", SdioWlOnStep())
  ; THE RUNG IN WORDS, EVERY RUN, PASS OR FAIL. The first two bench runs
  ; printed a bare number and the number had to be looked up in a report
  ; the person at the board did not have. A diagnostic that emits an
  ; integer nobody can decode has not diagnosed anything.
  TextLine("WL_ON meaning    ", SdioWlOnStepText())
  ; And the transport's own verdict at that rung. This line separates
  ; "the firmware refused the tag" from "the firmware never answered",
  ; which are the two halves of every mailbox failure and lead to
  ; completely different places.
  Line("WL_ON mailbox err", SdioWlOnMailboxError())
  TextLine("WL_ON mbx meaning", SdioMailboxErrorText(SdioWlOnMailboxError()))
  ; State as found, before this program wrote anything to the pin.
  ; Direction 0 is RPI_EXP_GPIO_DIR_IN and 1 is RPI_EXP_GPIO_DIR_OUT -
  ; expander driver lines 22-23. A level as found of 1 means the radio
  ; was already powered and a full cycle was performed; 0 means the pin
  ; was only ever driven high, so nothing could have left the radio
  ; worse off than it started.
  Line("WL_ON dir as found", SdioWlOnDirectionFound())
  Line("WL_ON level as found", SdioWlOnLevelFound())
  Line("WL_ON polarity as found", SdioWlOnPolarity())
  ; SET_GPIO_CONFIG is advisory now - the board refused it while
  ; accepting every other expander tag on the same pin. Its outcome is
  ; recorded and nothing waits for it.
  Line("WL_ON set-config ok", SdioWlOnConfigOk())
  TextLine("WL_ON set-config why", SdioMailboxErrorText(SdioWlOnConfigError()))
  ; THE DISCRIMINATOR FOR THE OPEN QUESTION. See AN OPEN QUESTION in
  ; sdio.pi4. A response length of 0 beside an MBX_ERR_TAG means the
  ; firmware returned nothing, and a cleared response bit on an empty
  ; reply may be a success we are misreading, because mailbox.pi4
  ; checks every individual tag's bit and is stricter than U-Boot.
  ; A length of 8 beside MBX_ERR_TAG is not that case.
  Line("WL_ON set-cfg resp bytes", SdioWlOnConfigRespBytes())
  Line("WL_ON set-state resp bytes", SdioWlOnSetRespBytes())
  ; -1 HERE MEANS THE READBACK NEVER RAN. It is not a pin level. The
  ; ladder stopped before rung 13 and the value was never fetched; the
  ; meaning line above says where it stopped.
  Line("WL_ON level readback (want 1)", SdioWlOnLevel())
  If SdioWlOnLevel() < 0
    UartWriteStr("    a readback of -1 is not a pin level: the")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("    GET_GPIO_STATE at the end of the ladder never ran")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  the radio may be unpowered; CMD5 will find nothing")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 2 - the clocks: the rate, and then whether it is actually running
  ; ====================================================================
  hz = MailboxClockRate(#PROBE_CLOCK_ID_EMMC)
  Line("EMMC clock Hz (id 1)", hz)
  If hz <= 0
    UartWriteStr("  the firmware would not name the EMMC clock - sdio.pi4 will refuse")
    PrintNl()
  Else
    khz = hz / 1000
    SdioSetBaseClockKhz(khz)
    Line("base clock kHz", khz)
  EndIf

  ; A RATE IS NOT A RUNNING CLOCK. The property document is explicit at
  ; line 310: "Next enable rate should be returned even if the clock is
  ; not running". The first bench run printed 250000000 for id 1 and
  ; that number carried no information about whether the controller was
  ; being clocked at all. These three lines carry it.
  Line("EMMC clock state bit0=on", SdioQueryClockState(#PROBE_CLOCK_ID_EMMC))
  Line("EMMC clock Hz measured", MailboxClockRateMeasured(#PROBE_CLOCK_ID_EMMC))
  ; The control: EMMC2's clock, feeding a controller known to work. This
  ; is a STATE query and never a rate query. sdio.pi4's base clock comes
  ; from id 1 and only from id 1 - that is the rule
  ; tools/a64/a64_sdio_check.py enforces, and asking id 12 what its rate
  ; is would break it. Asking whether it is running does not.
  Line("EMMC2 clock state (control)", SdioQueryClockState(#PROBE_CLOCK_ID_EMMC2))

  ; THE TWO ROWS ABOVE CONTRADICTED EACH OTHER ON 2026-08-26: state 0,
  ; measured 250000496 Hz. A measurement is the firmware counting real
  ; edges, and 496 Hz of jitter off a nominal 250 MHz is what a real
  ; measurement of a real clock looks like. A gated clock cannot be
  ; measured at 250.000496 MHz. So on this part the state bit appears to
  ; mean "some client has claimed this clock", not "the generator is
  ; running" - and the power sweep above says the same thing far more
  ; bluntly, by reporting UART0 as powered off in a message UART0 is
  ; transmitting. Both readings are in sdio.pi4, above
  ; sdio_EnableClockAndPower, with the argument spelled out.
  UartWriteStr("  if state says off and measured says a real rate, believe")
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("  the measurement - see sdio_EnableClockAndPower in sdio.pi4")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 2b - the raw registers, BEFORE anything judges them
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- raw registers, before anything judges them --")
  PrintNl()
  probe_RawRegisters()
  UartWriteStr("  a zero cap0 with a good ver is NORMAL here - see sdio.pi4")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 3 - the SDIO bus
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- sdio bring-up --")
  PrintNl()

  ok = SdioInit()
  Line("SdioInit (want 1)", ok)

  ; ALWAYS, BEFORE THE PASS/FAIL BRANCH. SdioInit runs the firmware half
  ; internally, and the first bench run could not tell whether the
  ; SET_CLOCK_STATE inside it had been skipped, refused or ignored -
  ; only that the state had not moved. Printing this on both paths is
  ; what makes those three distinguishable.
  probe_FirmwareHalfReport()

  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(SdioErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    HexLine("last cmd       ", SdioLastCmd(), 2)
    HexLine("last intr      ", SdioLastIntr(), 8)
    ; DECODED, EVERY TIME. See probe_DecodeIntr.
    probe_DecodeIntr(SdioLastIntr())
    HexLine("last state     ", SdioLastStatus(), 8)
    HexLine("last r5        ", SdioLastR5(), 4)
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("  -- the bus, as actually programmed --")
    PrintNl()
    probe_BusRegisters()
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the bus ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  UartWriteStr("  -- the bus, as actually programmed --")
  PrintNl()
  probe_BusRegisters()
  Line("host spec version", SdioHostVersion())
  Line("host vendor      ", SdioHostVendor())
  HexLine("capabilities 0   ", SdioCaps0(), 8)
  HexLine("capabilities 1   ", SdioCaps1(), 8)
  Line("bus clock kHz    ", SdioClockKhz())
  Line("clock divisor    ", SdioDivisor())
  Line("bus width        ", SdioBusWidth())
  HexLine("io ocr           ", SdioOcr(), 6)
  Line("io functions     ", SdioFunctionCount())
  Line("memory present   ", SdioMemoryPresent())
  Line("1.8V capable     ", Sdio18vCapable())
  HexLine("rca              ", SdioRca(), 4)
  ; REPORTED, NOT ACTED ON. The device on this controller is soldered
  ; down and the node is marked non-removable; these two bits are here
  ; because a surprising value in them is evidence about which
  ; controller is answering. See sdio_ControllerPlausible.
  Line("card detect bit  ", SdioCardDetectBit())
  Line("card pin level   ", SdioCardPinLevel())
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 4 - the two I/O functions
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- sdio functions --")
  PrintNl()

  ok = SdioEnableFunction(1)
  Line("enable F1 (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(SdioErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at function 1 ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ok = SdioEnableFunction(2)
  Line("enable F2 (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(SdioErrorText())
    PrintNl()
  EndIf

  ; A CCCR read that proves byte access works before the CYW43 layer
  ; depends on it. If this is wrong, everything above it lied.
  HexLine("CCCR revision    ", SdioCmd52Read(0, $00), 2)
  HexLine("CCCR caps        ", SdioCmd52Read(0, $08), 2)
  HexLine("CCCR bus if      ", SdioCmd52Read(0, $07), 2)
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 5 - the radio
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- cyw43 --")
  PrintNl()

  ok = Cyw43SetIo(@SdioCmd52Read, @SdioCmd52Write, @SdioCmd53Read, @SdioCmd53Write)
  Line("Cyw43SetIo (want 1)", ok)

  ok = Cyw43Attach()
  Line("Cyw43Attach (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    UartWriteStr("  ")
    UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    HexLine("chipclkcsr       ", Cyw43ClockCsr(), 2)
    HexLine("chipid raw       ", Cyw43ChipIdRaw(), 8)
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped at the chip ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  HexLine("chipclkcsr       ", Cyw43ClockCsr(), 2)
  HexLine("chipid raw       ", Cyw43ChipIdRaw(), 8)
  HexLine("chip id          ", Cyw43ChipId(), 4)
  Line("chip rev         ", Cyw43ChipRev())
  Line("chip package     ", Cyw43ChipPackage())
  Line("backplane type   ", Cyw43SocType())
  Line("chipid corecount ", Cyw43ChipCoreCountField())
  Line("is CYW43455      ", Cyw43IsCyw43455())
  TextLine("chip name        ", Cyw43ChipName())
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 5b - the cores
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- cores --")
  PrintNl()
  n = Cyw43CoreCount()
  Line("cores found      ", n)
  For i = 0 To n - 1
    UartWriteStr("  core ")
    PrintDec(i)
    UartWriteStr(" id $")
    PutHexN(Cyw43CoreId(i), 3)
    UartWriteStr(" rev ")
    PrintDec(Cyw43CoreRev(i))
    UartWriteStr(" base $")
    PutHexN(Cyw43CoreBase(i), 8)
    UartWriteStr(" wrap $")
    PutHexN(Cyw43CoreWrap(i), 8)
    PrintNl()
  Next
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 5c - BLOCK-MODE CMD53, PROVED AGAINST BYTE MODE
  ;
  ; This is the point of today's work and it is the one section of this
  ; program that is an EXPERIMENT rather than a report.
  ;
  ; THE TARGET IS THE EROM TABLE and the choice matters. Two transfer
  ; modes can only be compared over a region that reads the same twice,
  ; and most of this chip does not: ChipCommon's low 256 bytes contain
  ; live counters, the SDIO core's block contains interrupt status, and
  ; the chip's RAM is not writable until the CR4 is out of reset. The
  ; EROM is a descriptor table the silicon does not change, it is
  ; hundreds of bytes long, and cyw43.pi4 has already walked it word by
  ; word and got a coherent answer out of it - so a mismatch here is
  ; block mode's fault and nothing else's.
  ;
  ; WHAT EACH OUTCOME MEANS, because a bare "mismatches 0" is not a
  ; finding:
  ;
  ;   byte  ok 1, block ok 1, mismatches 0
  ;       Block-mode CMD53 works. This is the result the firmware
  ;       upload is waiting on.
  ;
  ;   block ok 0
  ;       The command itself failed. SdioLastError says which - a
  ;       DATA_TMO on block 1 means the controller never raised Buffer
  ;       Read Ready for a multi-block command, which would mean
  ;       SDHCI_TRNS_MULTI or the block count register is wrong. A
  ;       DATA_TMO on a LATER block means the per-block re-arm is wrong,
  ;       which is a different bug in the same procedure.
  ;
  ;   block ok 1, mismatches non-zero
  ;       The worst and most interesting outcome: the transfer completed
  ;       and moved the wrong bytes. Almost certainly the card and the
  ;       controller disagree about the block size. Compare the F1 block
  ;       size row above with 64.
  ;
  ;   byte ok 0
  ;       Nothing to do with today's work - the bulk path is broken in
  ;       byte mode too, and every result below is meaningless.
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- cmd53 block mode --")
  PrintNl()

  Line("F1 block size    ", Cyw43F1BlockSize())
  ; THE NUMBER THAT WAS WRONG ON THE FIRST BENCH RUN. This code chunked
  ; byte-mode transfers at 512 and the card refused 256; the real
  ; ceiling is the FUNCTION's block size. It is printed on its own row
  ; so that it can never be wrong silently again.
  Line("byte-mode max    ", Cyw43ByteMax())
  Line("card can multi-block (want 1)", Cyw43MultiBlockCapable())
  HexLine("erom pointer     ", Cyw43EromPtr(), 8)

  ok = Cyw43SetBlockIo(@SdioCmd53ReadBlocks, @SdioCmd53WriteBlocks)
  Line("Cyw43SetBlockIo (want 1)", ok)

  ; ctl and bad both start at -1, meaning NOT COMPARED, and only a
  ; comparison that actually ran may set them to a count. See the note
  ; on the control below.
  ctl = -1
  bad = -1

  If Cyw43EromPtr() = 0
    UartWriteStr("  no EROM pointer - skipping the comparison")
    PrintNl()
  Else
    ; Arm A: byte mode. Forced on even though it is the default, so that
    ; this line is a statement of what ran rather than a reliance on a
    ; default that might change.
    Cyw43SetBlockMode(0)
    Line("block mode off (want 0)", Cyw43BlockMode())
    okA = Cyw43ReadMem(Cyw43EromPtr(), @probe_bufA[0], #PROBE_XFER_BYTES)
    Line("byte-mode read (want 1) ", okA)
    If okA = 0
      probe_XferFailed()
    Else
      HexLine("  first word      ", PeekN(@probe_bufA[0]), 8)
    EndIf

    ; ==================================================================
    ;  THE CONTROL, and it must be able to say "I did not run".
    ;
    ;  On 2026-08-26 this printed "control mismatches (want 0)  0" while
    ;  BOTH reads had failed and nothing whatsoever had been compared.
    ;  Zero mismatches out of zero bytes is arithmetically true and
    ;  completely misleading, and an instrument that reports success
    ;  when it never ran is worse than one that reports nothing.
    ;
    ;  So the counter starts at -1 and the row prints "not compared"
    ;  for it. A count is only ever written by a loop that had two
    ;  buffers of real data to walk.
    ; ==================================================================
    okB = 0
    If okA = 1
      okB = Cyw43ReadMem(Cyw43EromPtr(), @probe_bufB[0], #PROBE_XFER_BYTES)
      Line("byte-mode read again    ", okB)
      If okB = 0
        probe_XferFailed()
      EndIf
    Else
      UartWriteStr("  second byte-mode read SKIPPED - the first one failed")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf

    If okA = 1 And okB = 1
      ctl = 0
      For i = 0 To #PROBE_XFER_BYTES - 1
        If PeekA(@probe_bufA[0] + i) <> PeekA(@probe_bufB[0] + i)
          ctl = ctl + 1
        EndIf
      Next
    EndIf
    probe_Count("control mismatches (want 0)", ctl)
    If ctl > 0
      UartWriteStr("  THE REGION IS NOT STATIC. The comparison below cannot")
      PrintNl()
      UartWriteStr("  distinguish a block-mode fault from a changing register.")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf

    ; Arm B: block mode over the identical region. Only attempted when
    ; the control actually passed - a block-mode result measured against
    ; a byte-mode arm that never ran is the vacuous answer again, one
    ; layer up.
    If ctl <> 0
      UartWriteStr("  block-mode comparison SKIPPED - no usable control")
      PrintNl()
    Else
      okC = Cyw43SetBlockMode(1)
      Line("block mode on (want 1)  ", okC)
      If okC = 0
        UartWriteStr("    ")
        UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
        PrintNl()
      Else
        okC = Cyw43ReadMem(Cyw43EromPtr(), @probe_bufB[0], #PROBE_XFER_BYTES)
        Line("block-mode read (want 1)", okC)
        If okC = 0
          probe_XferFailed()
        Else
          HexLine("  first word      ", PeekN(@probe_bufB[0]), 8)
          bad = 0
          For i = 0 To #PROBE_XFER_BYTES - 1
            If PeekA(@probe_bufA[0] + i) <> PeekA(@probe_bufB[0] + i)
              If bad = 0
                Line("  first mismatch at byte", i)
                HexLine("    byte mode said  ", PeekA(@probe_bufA[0] + i), 2)
                HexLine("    block mode said ", PeekA(@probe_bufB[0] + i), 2)
              EndIf
              bad = bad + 1
            EndIf
          Next
          Line("bytes compared   ", #PROBE_XFER_BYTES)
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf
    probe_Count("mismatches (want 0)", bad)
    ; Leave block mode ON for the steps below if it worked, because that
    ; is the configuration the upload will use and a diagnostic that
    ; tests one thing and then reports on another is worse than no
    ; diagnostic.
  EndIf
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 5d - THE CORES GO DOWN AND THE CHIP SAYS HOW MUCH RAM IT HAS
  ;
  ; brcmf_chip_cr4_set_passive() then brcmf_chip_get_raminfo(), in that
  ; order and not the other one - the CR4's bank registers only answer
  ; once the CR4 itself is out of reset and halted.
  ;
  ; THIS WRITES TO THE CHIP. Everything above it was read-only. What it
  ; does is put the radio's own ARM into a halted state, which is where
  ; it already is after a power-on, so the worst case is a chip that
  ; needs the WL_ON cycle this program already performs at step 1.
  ;
  ; WHAT THE NUMBERS SHOULD BE. RAM base $00198000, from
  ; brcmf_chip_tcm_rambase()'s table for a $4345. RAM size is WALKED out
  ; of the CR4's memory banks and is not predicted here on purpose - a
  ; predicted number is a number this program would find a way to agree
  ; with. It must be non-zero, a multiple of 8192 or 1024, and comfortably
  ; larger than the 609,309-byte image; a 43455 is generally reported as
  ; having 786,432 bytes.
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- cores down, ram info --")
  PrintNl()

  Line("ARM CR4 core index", Cyw43FindCore(#CYW43_CORE_ARM_CR4))
  Line("802.11 core index ", Cyw43FindCore(#CYW43_CORE_80211))
  Line("SDIO dev core index", Cyw43FindCore(#CYW43_CORE_SDIO_DEV))

  ok = Cyw43PrepareDownload()
  Line("Cyw43PrepareDownload (want 1)", ok)
  If ok = 0
    ; BOTH LAYERS, and the SDIO one first in your reading order. On
    ; 2026-08-26 this printed only "a 32-bit backplane read failed",
    ; which sent the reader to the backplane window when the actual
    ; fault was a data line left inhibited by a refused command three
    ; sections earlier. The bus layer knew; nobody asked it.
    probe_XferFailed()
    UartWriteStr("=== stopped before the download ===")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  Line("CR4 is up (want 1)", Cyw43CoreIsUp(Cyw43FindCore(#CYW43_CORE_ARM_CR4)))
  HexLine("ram base (want $198000)", Cyw43RamBase(), 8)
  Line("ram size bytes   ", Cyw43RamSize())
  HexLine("ram size hex     ", Cyw43RamSize(), 8)
  ; The image is 609,309 bytes. Printed as a comparison rather than as a
  ; verdict, because the number that matters is the one the chip
  ; reported and a "yes it fits" line would hide it.
  Line("image bytes needed", 609309)
  Line("ram size minus image", Cyw43RamSize() - 609309)
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 5e - THE CHIP'S RAM, WRITTEN AND READ BACK
  ;
  ; The first WRITE of bulk data this project has ever made to this
  ; chip, and the last thing that can be proved without the 609 KB image
  ; on the board. A pattern goes into the top of the image area, comes
  ; back, and is compared.
  ;
  ; WHY IT IS SAFE: the ARM is halted and there is no firmware in that
  ; RAM to corrupt. The region used is the bottom of the image area,
  ; which the upload will overwrite in its entirety anyway.
  ;
  ; IF THIS FAILS AND 5c PASSED, the fault is in the WRITE direction
  ; specifically - reads were already proven. Re-run with block mode off
  ; to find out whether it is block mode's write path or the bulk path
  ; as a whole.
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- ram write and read back --")
  PrintNl()

  For i = 0 To #PROBE_XFER_BYTES - 1
    ; A pattern that is different in every byte AND different from any
    ; plausible stuck value: not all-zero, not all-ones, and not a
    ; repeating byte, so that an off-by-one in the block arithmetic
    ; shows up as a mismatch rather than as more of the same.
    PokeB(@probe_bufA[0] + i, (i * 7 + 19) & $FF)
  Next

  ok = Cyw43WriteMem(Cyw43RamBase(), @probe_bufA[0], #PROBE_XFER_BYTES)
  Line("Cyw43WriteMem (want 1)", ok)
  ; bad stays at -1 - NOT COMPARED - unless a verify pass actually ran.
  ; Cyw43VerifyMem returns -1 for "the read-back itself failed" for the
  ; same reason, so the two meanings coincide and probe_Count prints
  ; words rather than a number in both.
  bad = -1
  If ok = 0
    probe_XferFailed()
  Else
    Cyw43ClearVerifyRecord()
    bad = Cyw43VerifyMem(Cyw43RamBase(), @probe_bufA[0], #PROBE_XFER_BYTES, @probe_bufB[0], #PROBE_XFER_BYTES)
    If bad < 0
      UartWriteStr("  the read-back itself failed:")
      PrintNl()
      probe_XferFailed()
    EndIf
    If bad > 0
      HexLine("  first bad address", Cyw43BadAddr(), 8)
      HexLine("  chip said        ", Cyw43BadGot(), 2)
      HexLine("  should have been ", Cyw43BadWant(), 2)
    EndIf
  EndIf
  probe_Count("ram mismatches (want 0)", bad)
  PrintNl()

  ; ====================================================================
  ; 6 - the firmware, which this program does not carry
  ;
  ; NOT PADDING. This is the finding. Everything above it is bring-up
  ; that leads here, and a log that stopped at "ram size" would let a
  ; reader assume the rest is a matter of time.
  ;
  ; THE REFUSAL THAT USED TO BE HERE IS GONE, and it is worth saying why
  ; in the log itself rather than only in a source comment: the three
  ; image and CLM blob are in the repository at Firmware/CYW43455/ and
  ; the licence permits it. What stops THIS program is arithmetic, not
  ; permission - 609,309 bytes will not fit in a diagnostic image, so
  ; they have to arrive at run time, and choosing how is a separate job
  ; with its own risks. See the header.
  ;
  ; Cyw43Start is called anyway, and it must return 0 with
  ; #CYW43_ERR_NOT_LOADED - "releasing the ARM needs BOTH a verified
  ; image and a verified NVRAM". A 1 here would mean the guard is
  ; broken and the radio's ARM had just been released into empty RAM.
  ; ====================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- firmware --")
  PrintNl()
  Line("firmware required (want 1)", Cyw43FirmwareRequired())
  TextLine("image needed     ", Cyw43FirmwareName())
  TextLine("nvram needed     ", Cyw43NvramName())
  TextLine("clm (optional)   ", Cyw43ClmName())
  Line("Cyw43Start with nothing loaded (want 0)", Cyw43Start())
  UartWriteStr("  ")
  UartWriteStr(Cyw43ErrorText())
  PrintNl()
  ; SCANNING IS NO LONGER ON THIS LIST. Cyw43ScanStart became real on
  ; 2026-08-27 with the SDPCM transport, so calling it here would start
  ; a scan on a radio with no firmware in it rather than print a
  ; refusal. What it needs is the image, which this program has no way
  ; to obtain - see the header. The scan diagnostic is
  ; pi4WifiScan.pi4.
  ;
  ; JOINING LEFT THIS LIST TOO, ON THE EVENING OF 2026-08-27.
  ; Cyw43Join was the last stub standing in for the whole refused set
  ; and it is gone: the security block, the event ring and the join
  ; state machine are section 8 of cyw43.pi4, and the board associated
  ; with a real access point on the strength of them. Calling it here
  ; would now ATTEMPT a join on a radio with no firmware in it rather
  ; than print a refusal - exactly the trap Cyw43ScanStart left this
  ; list for that morning. The join diagnostic is pi4WifiJoin.pi4.
  ;
  ; SO THE REFUSAL LIST IS EMPTY AND THIS PROGRAM NO LONGER TESTS ONE.
  ; Cyw43Start above is what remains and it is the right survivor: it
  ; is the one call that still MUST refuse without an image, and its
  ; guard is the one whose failure would release the radio's ARM into
  ; empty RAM.
  PrintNl()

  UartWriteStr("=== done ===")
  PrintNl()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
